/* PMC 2670 — Firefight : Dead Reckoning's words (docs/story-dead-reckoning-plan.md, phase 3)

   The story's prose, kept apart from its working (dead-reckoning.js), as
   Deep Contract's is. Keyed by what it belongs to:
     a chapter's id           its intro, on the chapter's card
     a node's id              its text (a brief, an event, a debrief, an interlude)
     'node/option'            an option's text
     'node/option.why'        why an option is closed
     'node#name'              a battle's mission name
   Anything not here keeps the one-liner in dead-reckoning.js. Text can name
   the company's own units ({unit:…}, {soldier:…}), the company ({name}), its
   commander ({commander}, {commander:rank}), and say one thing or another by
   what has happened ({if flag}…{/if}; `success` and `failure` are the last
   battle's, `relic` that the company came away with it).

   Written in full: both halves, both one-offs, Recall, and the events shared
   between chapters. In the first half nothing names the Xenotripods; the
   second opens on the name (docs/story-dead-reckoning-plan.md, section 0). */
(function (root) {
  'use strict';
  root.PMCStoryText = root.PMCStoryText || {};
  root.PMCStoryText['dead-reckoning'] = {

    /* ================= 1. Lot 47 ================= */
    c1: 'Forty years ago a surveyor called Elias Morrow flew past the edge of the charts on somebody else’s money, and came home with a hold full of carved stones that no human hand had made, and a log nobody could read. Within the year he had stolen the stones back from the man who paid for them and gone out again, alone, with that man’s people never more than a jump or two behind him, putting the stones down one at a time along the way and sending his log home by relay, until the entries stopped. Nobody has heard from him since; a court declared him dead a long time ago. Tonight the log comes up for auction in Vesk Free Port as Lot 47. Halloran has come to bid on it. He is an archaeologist who works in archives, not dig sites, and he has spent years on the scraps of Morrow’s writing that have turned up since. Nobody reads Morrow better. He has the money to bid with, from a bank on Halcyon and a backer he does not name, and he has hired {name} to see that he leaves the room with the log, and leaves the port alive.',
    'c1.brief': 'Vesk Free Port is a market with a landing field attached. The auction house stands on the square, its windows lit, its doors guarded by hired muscle. Halloran walks in with an earpiece in, talking quietly to somebody he does not name. A few heads turn.',
    'c1.bidding': 'Lot 47 is a battered data slate and a bundle of handwritten pages, and half the room wants it, but only one man is really bidding against Halloran: Moss Kettering of Brass Ledger, in the front row, raising every bid as soon as it is made. The rest drop out one by one, except two at the back who are bidding for somebody else and checking a screen between bids. Halloran listens to his earpiece, nods, and says to nobody in particular: “Whatever it takes.”',
    'c1.bidding/outbid': 'Stand with Halloran while he bids, in plain sight. Let the room see whose company it is, and let his backer see it too.',
    'c1.bidding/watch': 'Stay in the background and observe: who leaves early, who signals, who checks their weapons.',
    'c1.bidding/follow': 'Focus on Moss Kettering, and mark his men in the room, one by one. When they move, move first.',
    'c1.battle#name': 'Lot 47',
    'c1.battle': 'Halloran comes out of the auction house with the slate in his coat and the pages under his arm, and a face the colour of paper. “I have it,” he says. “Now get me out of here.” The company’s ship is on the landing field on the far side of the market square.{if caught_out} The company is strung out along the west road from the auction house, Halloran in the middle of the column, when there is a crack of gunfire from the stalls across the square, and then a great deal more. Ambush! Brass Ledger are in the doorways and behind the market stalls, and they knew exactly who to wait for.{/if}{if room_watched} The company watched Kettering’s men slip out of the room one at a time before the hammer fell: to the arcade, the fountain, the stalls. So when the first shot cracks out across the square, nobody is surprised. It is an ambush, and the company knows where it is.{/if}{if kettle_marked} Kettering’s men slipped out of the room before the hammer fell, to lie in wait for Halloran in the square, and the company had somebody on every one of them. They are still gathering behind the stalls at the far end of the square when the company opens fire.{/if} Get him across. Halloran is no soldier, but he will go where the company tells him. Brass Ledger have a lot of men, none of them much good, and they are only after Halloran.',
    'c1.debrief': '{if success}Brass Ledger melt back into the market, leaving their wounded for the port to deal with. Halloran walks up the ramp of the company’s ship with the log under his arm, and the line in his ear says: “Good.”{/if}{if success+caught_out} Then, after a moment: “They walked into that and still got you out. There will be something extra in their account for it.”{/if}{if failure}Brass Ledger have the log. They took it off Halloran in the arcade, and the company could not take it back. Halloran got away with his notes and the first pages photographed, and nothing else. On the line in Halloran’s ear, his backer says nothing for a long time.{/if}',
    'c1.agent': 'One of Kettering’s people was left behind in the square when Brass Ledger pulled back: the one who had been watching the auction-house door all evening. He is in the back of the company’s transport with his hands tied. He is young and frightened, and keeps saying he was only paid to watch. Within the hour Kettering is on the line, wanting him back.',
    'c1.agent/message': 'Let him go, and send a message back to Kettering with him.',
    'c1.agent/interrogate': 'Question him hard first, then let him go.',
    'c1.agent/authorities': 'Hand him to the port authorities, and make sure Halloran’s backer knows you did.',
    'c1.lost': 'From the arcade roof the company can see Brass Ledger’s ship on the landing field, its ramp down and its engines warming, and Kettering’s people loading it in a hurry. The log is aboard. In an hour it will be a jump away, and in a week it will be for sale.',
    'c1.lost/raid': 'Hit their ship before it lifts, and take the log back off it.',
    'c1.lost/cutin': 'Cut Kettering in on the claim: the log back now, and Brass Ledger’s name beside the company’s when the claim is filed. Halloran’s backer will not like sharing.',
    'c1.lost/follow': 'Let them run with it, and follow. Halloran has the first pages; Brass Ledger will lead the company to the rest. Somewhere on the trail there will be another way to put it back together.',
    'c1r.battle#name': 'Taking It Back',
    'c1r.battle': 'Brass Ledger are loading their ship on Vesk’s landing field, and the log is somewhere in the loading: on the ramp, in the cargo stacks, or still in the customs shed. Find it and carry it off the field across your own edge before the ship lifts. Kettering’s people know they have won, and they are in a hurry.',
    'c1r.debrief': '{if relic}The log is back in the company’s hands, a little scorched. Brass Ledger’s ship lifts off empty, and Kettering does not call.{/if}{if not relic}Brass Ledger’s ship lifts off the field with the log aboard. Halloran watches it go with his photographs in his hand.{/if}',
    'c1.interlude': '{if not ledger_has_log}The company lifts off Vesk with the log in its strongroom and Morrow’s first heading on the navigator’s board. Morrow wrote no star fixes, only headings, times and what he saw. Halloran has been reading it for a week, and the other readers his backer pays disagree with him about where it starts.{/if}{if ledger_has_log}The company lifts off Vesk without the log. Halloran has the first pages, photographed in the arcade, and Brass Ledger have everything else. Kettering will copy it and sell it, a page at a time, to anyone who pays. Wherever Brass Ledger go next, the company will have to follow.{/if}',

    /* ================= 2. The First Lodestone ================= */
    c2: 'Morrow’s first entry is a heading, a burn time, and a line: “Glass under me for a day and a night. Put the first stone where I could find it again.” Halloran has found two places it could mean. The company can only follow one, and whoever follows the other will not be sharing.',
    'c2.brief': 'Two readings of the first heading, a day apart by jump, and both of them wrong in places. Halloran’s backer leaves the choice to {commander:rank}. “You’re the one who has to dig,” says the voice on the line.',
    'c2.readings': 'One reading puts Morrow at a dig on a desert world called Vantor III, where a meteor once turned a plain to glass. The other puts him at Station Thule, a survey relay in orbit over Okoro II, where his log stops for three days. Sable Armour Group has chartered a ship for Okoro. Somebody quieter has been asking about Vantor.{if ledger_has_log} Both readings are already for sale: Brass Ledger have printed them on the back of a catalogue.{/if}',
    'c2.readings/flats': 'The Glass Flats. If Morrow dug, there’s something buried.',
    'c2.readings/thule': 'Station Thule. If he stopped for three days, he left something behind.',

    'c2f.dig': 'The Glass Flats shine like a sheet of ice under two suns. Morrow’s dig is a ring of rusted marker posts, half buried. Inside it, under a cairn of fused glass, two bodies lie side by side: small, armoured, not human, and laid out with care a long time ago. Wraith Section is already camped beside it: a dozen low tents, a mast, and no sentries anyone can see. Nine, who leads them, sends a message on the open channel. It says: “We have read the log too. We hold the dig. Turn back, or be fired on.”',
    'c2f.dig/night': 'Dig at night, when the glass is cold and the snipers can’t see far.',
    'c2f.dig/jam': 'Jam their comms first. Wraith is nothing without its listening.',
    'c2f.dig/jam.why': 'There is no Intel to spend on it.',
    'c2f.dig/rush': 'Go straight for the dig, now, before they’re ready.',
    'c2f.battle#name': 'The Glass Flats',
    'c2f.battle': 'Three places on the flats could be Morrow’s dig: a ring of marker posts, a half-buried shelter, a scorched hollow in the glass. One holds the lodestone. Search them, dig it out, and get it off the flats: carried home across your own edge, it is yours whatever else happens. Wraith Section fights from far away, and does not miss often. The gravity is light here, and everything moves further than it should.',
    'c2f.debrief': '{if relic}The lodestone is yours: a warm, dull stone the size of a palm, carved on every face in lines that are not writing, or not any writing anybody knows. Set down on the chart table, it turns, slowly, until one carved face points somewhere off the edge of the charts. Halloran writes the bearing down beside Morrow’s next heading. They agree.{/if}{if not relic}You come away from the flats without the lodestone.{/if}',
    'c2f.drop': 'Wraith Section left in a hurry. Their camp still has a dead drop in it: a sealed case with their orders inside, and their copy of the company’s copy of the log.{if ledger_has_log} It is Brass Ledger’s copy, bought by Wraith a page at a time, and with Halloran’s photographs it is very nearly the whole log.{/if}',
    'c2f.drop/read': 'Read their orders. Find out who they work for, and what they know.',
    'c2f.drop/warn': 'Leave a message in the drop, for Nine, and leave the rest alone.',
    'c2f.drop/take': 'Take back their stolen copy of the log.',
    'c2f.second': 'The stone got away from you. Whoever has it now is a step ahead, and they know you know.',
    'c2f.second/chase': 'Follow their dropship’s trail while it’s fresh. The company can rest later.',
    'c2f.second/dig': 'Let it go, and dig on. Morrow may have left more than one thing here.',

    'c2t.airlock': 'Station Thule hangs in orbit over Okoro II, a cross of modules and docking arms turning slowly in the dark, its solar wings catching the sun. It still belongs to the survey office, on paper. Every docking arm has been welded shut from inside, and somebody has taken it anyway: scavengers, by the junk drifting round the arms, squatting on a station that was never theirs to claim, and enough of them to have opinions about visitors.',
    'c2t.airlock/cut': 'Cut through a docking arm’s seal and go in hard.',
    'c2t.airlock/hatch': 'Find Morrow’s maintenance hatch. His log mentions it, twice.',
    'c2t.airlock/pay': 'Pay for access to the archive. Nobody needs to die for a relay.',
    'c2t.airlock/pay.why': 'The company can’t pay them.',
    'c2t.battle#name': 'Station Thule',
    'c2t.battle': 'Station Thule is corridors and modules sealed against the vacuum, a docking arm at each corner. Whoever holds its three control rooms holds the station: the hub in the middle, where the relay’s archive keeps Morrow’s three days, and the north and south modules that run its power and its air. The scavengers hold it now and know every corner of it. Take control of the station: two of the rooms at the end, or all three at once, and keep them. There is no air outside the hull, and nowhere to run to.',
    'c2t.debrief': '{if skip}The scavengers count the money twice, and open the lock. They walk the company to the hub and back with their guns never quite pointed at anyone, and let it copy the archive: three days of Morrow, talking to himself in an empty room. On the desk in front of the microphone is the stone he was talking to, still there. Mags shrugs at it: a paperweight, as far as she knows, and thrown in with the price. On the company’s chart table it turns, slowly, until it points somewhere off the charts.{/if}{if success}The station is the company’s, and so is the archive: three days of Morrow, talking to himself in an empty room, and on the desk in front of the microphone the stone he was talking to, still there. It turns on the desk, slowly, until it points somewhere off the charts.{/if}{if failure}The scavengers fired the archive on their way out. The air in the control room still smells of it.{/if}',
    'c2t.records': 'The relay kept everything Morrow said for three days. Most of it is to himself. Some of it is to the stone on the desk in front of him. Some of it is to two others the relay did not record, whom he calls Holds-the-Lamp and First-Over-the-Water, and thanks, and tells to keep their heads down. He sounds frightened, and twice he breaks off to listen to the relay’s traffic and says, to nobody: “The same ship. Two jumps behind me now.” Then, towards the end, he sounds happy, like a man who has decided something.{if ledger_has_log} He reads his headings aloud, every one, as if he knew someone would need them. With Halloran’s photographs, it is the log again.{/if}',
    'c2t.records/listen': 'Listen to all of it, every hour, with the company.',
    'c2t.records/send': 'Send the records to Halloran’s backer. He’s paying for them.',
    'c2t.records/sell': 'Sell a copy to a newsfeed. Someone will pay well for a dead man’s voice.',
    'c2t.burned': 'The scavengers fired the archive. The racks are melted, but not all of them, and the company has a man who used to repair this kind of thing.',
    'c2t.burned/sift': 'Sift the ashes for whatever survived.',
    'c2t.burned/go': 'Take what’s left of the records and go. The rivals aren’t waiting.',
    'c2.interlude': 'The company jumps on. Whatever Morrow found at his first stop, it pointed him to a world of water and trees, and a thing standing in the water that he called the beacon.',

    /* ================= 3. The Drowned Beacon ================= */
    c3: 'Tessaly IV is a world of drowned forest under a permanent fog. Morrow’s beacon stands in the middle of it, half under water: a spire of something like stone and something like glass, older than anything humans have built, and humming. Nobody built it that anybody knows of. Morrow called it a beacon because it was lit. It is still lit. And the Red Reavers, who turn up wherever somebody else has done the work, are already digging at its foot.',
    'c3.brief': 'The landing craft sets down on a mud bank a mile from the beacon. The fog is thick enough to taste. The Reavers’ dig is lit up like a fairground, all floodlights and generators, and the instruments say there is something else moving in the fog: and that it stopped moving when the company landed.',
    'c3.sale': 'Down the bank, half hidden in the reeds, is a ship the company knows: Brass Ledger’s, from Vesk. The Reavers’ boats are pulled up beside it, and under a lit tent between them Kettering and Big Hal are haggling over a scuffed case. Morrow’s log has come to the beacon it points at, to be sold to the people digging there.',
    'c3.sale/raid': 'Break up the sale, and take the case off the table.',
    'c3.sale/wait': 'Let it go through. Kettering will only sell them a copy; the original is going to Kerrigan.',
    'c3s.battle#name': 'The Private Sale',
    'c3s.battle': 'A mud bank in the fog, Brass Ledger’s ship in the reeds, and the log somewhere in the sale: on the ship, on the Reavers’ boats, or still on the table in the tent. Find it, and carry it off the bank across your own edge. Kettering’s people are guarding their goods, and nobody here wants the beacon’s keepers to hear the shooting.',
    'c3s.debrief': '{if relic}The case is the company’s, and Morrow’s log is in it, every page. Kettering and Hal are still blaming each other when the company lifts off the bank.{/if}{if not relic}The sale goes through. Hal pays for his copy, and Kettering’s ship lifts with the original, for Kerrigan.{/if}',
    'c3.contact': 'Big Hal Reaver has a crew of thirty in the shallows with cutting gear, going at the foot of the spire. He has seen the company land, and sends a message on the open channel: first come, first served, and he came first.',
    'c3.contact/storm': 'Storm the dig, now, while they’re still arguing about who you are.',
    'c3.contact/watch': 'Watch first. Learn the Reavers’ lines, and what else is moving out there.',
    'c3.contact/signal': 'Signal the beacon itself, on every band, as Morrow did. He got in and out alive.',
    'c3.battle#name': 'The Drowned Beacon',
    'c3.battle': 'Three places on the beacon’s island could hold its lodestone: the socket at the foot of the spire, a sunken shrine, a ring of standing stones in the shallows. One does. Find it, prise it loose, and carry it out across your own edge, or hold it when the fog closes in. The Reavers are between the company and all three. And whatever is moving in the fog has not finished deciding what to do about any of you.',
    'c3.debrief': '{if skip}The beacon answers the signal with one of its own, and the things in the fog let the company walk off the island without a shot, a rubbing of the beacon’s markings and a lodestone with it. The Reavers watched all of it, and are telling everyone who will listen.{/if}{if success}The beacon is quiet. The things that came out of the air have gone back into it; nobody saw how. The Reavers have gone too, and are telling everyone who will listen.{/if}{if failure}They drove the company off the island: tall, armoured, three-legged things that came out of nowhere and took no notice of a single shot. From the mud bank, the beacon is still lit.{/if}{if relic} The lodestone is in the company’s strongroom, and the beacon’s hum has dropped a note.{/if}',
    'c3.left': 'In the shelter of the spire, one of them is down: three legs folded under it, a carapace like wet ceramic, a slit of a helmet with lights in it that follow the company as it comes near. It is hurt, and it is not afraid. Above it, still in its socket, the beacon’s lodestone glows.',
    'c3.left/stone': 'Take the lodestone, and leave it where it lies.',
    'c3.left/leave': 'Leave the stone in its socket, and the hurt one with it.',
    'c3.left/both': 'Take both. Halloran’s backer will pay anything for one of them alive.',
    'c3.left/gate': 'The air beside the spire is still open where they stepped out of it. Step through.',
    'c3.left/gate.why': 'The air closed behind them.',
    'c3.copy': 'A message reaches the company in orbit, from Moss Kettering. Someone, he does not say who, would pay handsomely for a copy of the chart so far. He names a sum that would buy the company a great many things, and adds that if the chart is not for sale, his client has asked him to price the alternative.',
    'c3.copy/sell': 'Sell them a copy. A backer with no name hasn’t earned your loyalty.',
    'c3.copy/sell.why': 'You still work for Halloran’s backer, and he still trusts you.',
    'c3.copy/refuse': 'Refuse, and say nothing to Halloran’s backer.',
    'c3.copy/report': 'Refuse, and tell Halloran’s backer exactly who asked.',
    'c3.interlude': 'The company leaves Tessaly IV with the fog closing behind it, and nobody aboard says what they saw, because nobody has a word for it. Halloran’s backer, on the line, asks for a full report, and then for every frame of footage, and then says nothing at all for a long time. The next heading in Morrow’s log is crossed out, and beside it, in his own hand, cramped and hurried: “Sold one. Kerrigan. Fuel. God forgive me. Never two in one hold.”',

    /* ================= 4. The Broker ================= */
    c4: 'Running out of money and fuel, Morrow sold one of his stones on Kerrigan, forty years ago, to a dealer who never knew what it was. The dealer’s heirs have just found it, with Morrow’s bill of sale and the heading he wrote on it. Brass Ledger’s broker on Kerrigan is auctioning stone and heading together, to everyone, and Sable Armour Group has bought the warehouse they sit in.',
    'c4.brief': 'Kerrigan is docks and cranes and the smell of reaction mass. Sable Armour Group has parked its tanks in a warehouse on the waterfront and its colonel in the broker’s office. Everybody in the race has a seat at the broker’s table, and nobody has sat down.',
    'c4.lot': 'Colonel Agathe Sable does not bid. She has the warehouse, Morrow’s stone and its heading are in the warehouse, and her tanks are in front of it. She will let the broker sell the company the lot, at the broker’s price, and see it delivered. Anyone who tries to take one instead, she says, will be shot before they reach the door. It is not a threat; it is the terms of her contract.{if ledger_has_log} And on the broker’s table, beside the heading, is Lot 47 itself: Morrow’s log, under glass, with Brass Ledger’s seal on the case and a card that says it is not for sale at any price. It is in the strongroom with the heading; taking the one means taking the other.{/if}',
    'c4.lot/buy': 'Buy the stone and its heading, at the broker’s price.',
    'c4.lot/buy.why': 'The company can’t afford the broker’s price.',
    'c4.lot/take': 'Take the warehouse. Sable dares anyone to; dare back.',
    'c4.lot/take.why': 'The company is too worn out to go at Sable’s tanks. Rest them first.',
    'c4.lot/follow': 'Let Sable have it, and follow Sable wherever she goes next. Stand aside now, when it could cost her the dock, and Sable will remember it.',
    'c4.lot/hire_kettle': 'Hire Brass Ledger for the takeover. They know the docks.',
    'c4.lot/hire_kettle.why': 'Brass Ledger won’t fight for the company: they have no reason to, or they are still sitting on the log it wants back.',
    'c4.battle#name': 'The Broker’s Warehouse',
    'c4.battle': 'Sable’s warehouse stands in the middle of the docks, dug in and walled, with Morrow’s stone and its heading in its strongroom. Sable Armour Group fights from inside steel and does not move unless it has to. Take the warehouse and hold it, and the heading is the company’s; let Sable hold it, and she sells it to somebody else.',
    'c4.debrief': '{if skip+!c4_followed}The stone and its heading are the company’s, bought at the broker’s price, in a strongbox with Morrow’s bill of sale. The broker counts the money twice.{/if}{if skip+c4_followed}Sable takes the stone and its heading out of the warehouse, and the company lets her. Her ship lifts off Kerrigan with the company’s a respectful distance behind, and files its flight plan under the Halvorsen Mining Combine’s name.{/if}{if success}The warehouse is the company’s. The stone and its heading were in a strongbox in the office, behind the broker’s desk, with Morrow’s bill of sale, and the broker is very sorry. Sable’s papers are on the desk too: she works for the Halvorsen Mining Combine.{/if}{if failure}Sable keeps the warehouse, and the stone and its heading go to the highest bidder. The company was not it.{/if}',
    'c4.copies': 'The broker has been selling copies of the heading all week, to everyone who could pay. He has five left, and a ship to catch.',
    'c4.copies/burn': 'Burn his stock, in front of him.',
    'c4.copies/buyall': 'Buy every copy he has left.',
    'c4.copies/buyall.why': 'The company can’t afford them all.',
    'c4.copies/let': 'Let him go. Everybody has a copy anyway.',
    'c4.interlude': 'Morrow’s log runs out of easy headings at Kerrigan. From here on he wrote less and less, and what he wrote was mostly about the stones, which had started to point, and about the ones who keep them, whom he never names. Halloran reads the pages twice, and then asks {commander:rank}, quietly, how many stones the company is carrying now.',

    /* ================= Into the Warren (one-off) ================= */
    warren: 'The company stepped through the air at the beacon, and came out underground, on a world that is on nobody’s chart, in tunnels older than anything on the human record. The way behind is closing. The Red Reavers came through first, after whatever is down here, and they are not the only ones in the dark.',
    'warren.brief': 'The air is thick and warm and tastes of metal, and the instruments say there is radiation in the rock, not enough to kill quickly. The tunnels are high and wide, cut for something taller than a man, and something has been walking through them for a very long time.',
    'warren.other': 'Behind the company, the way back is shrinking: a ring of light in the rock, closing. Ahead, the tunnels branch, and somewhere down them the Reavers are shouting at each other. Under the shouting there is something else, not quite a sound, in a language that feels like a headache.',
    'warren.other/hold': 'Hold the gate open with charges. The company needs a way home.',
    'warren.other/close': 'Let it close. The company will find another way.',
    'warren.other/voices': 'Follow the other sound.',
    'warren.other/voices.why': 'The company is too worn out to go any deeper into the dark.',
    'warren.battle#name': 'Into the Warren',
    'warren.battle': 'The tunnels are corridors and chambers, warm, close and dark. The Reavers have dug in among them with the hoard they came for. Clear them, room by room, until the way out is open. And keep an eye on the walls: the Reavers keep firing at them.',
    'warren.debrief': '{if success}The company comes out the long way round: through a second ring of light in the rock, and into the fog of Tessaly IV, a mile from the beacon. Wherever it has been, it is on nobody’s chart. It had better start being on one.{/if}{if failure}The company gets out through a second ring of light in the rock, back into Tessaly’s fog, but not the way it went in, and not all of it.{/if}',
    'warren.hoard': 'Deep in the tunnels, past where the Reavers stopped, there is a chamber of relics: hundreds of them, in rows, as carefully kept as a library. Somebody keeps it. Among them is a lodestone that no rival will ever know about.',
    'warren.hoard/stone': 'Take the lodestone, and only the lodestone.',
    'warren.hoard/all': 'Take everything the company can carry.',
    'warren.hoard/mark': 'Take nothing, and mark the way here. Whoever keeps it should be asked first.',
    'warren.interlude': 'The company finds its ship where it left it, on the mud bank on Tessaly IV, two days and a whole world later. Nobody in the company talks about the warren much, and nobody forgets it.',

    /* ================= The Double-Cross (one-off) ================= */
    doublecross: 'Halloran’s backer has a company of his own: the Ninth Lance, as expensive as its name, and as hard to find on any register as he is. The few registers that list it give a founding date forty years back, and a first contract nobody remembers: a man to be found, somewhere past the edge of the charts. It docks beside the company’s ship on Kerrigan’s orbital with a court order in its commander’s hand, and its weapons hot.',
    'dx.brief': 'Commander Iselin Daw of the Ninth Lance comes aboard alone, with two pages of legal text and a very calm voice. Their employer has terminated the company’s contract for breach: he believes it has been selling what he paid for. The log is his property. The Lance has come to collect it.',
    'dx.repo': '“Hand back the log,” Daw says, “and you walk away with your pay to date. That’s generous, and Vane knows it. Refuse, and I take it. Fire on my people, and I won’t be the one who decides what happens next.”',
    'dx.repo/hand': 'Hand back the log. It isn’t worth the company.',
    'dx.repo/refuse': 'Refuse. If he wants it, he can come out of hiding and take it.',
    'dx.repo/fire': 'Fire first, while Daw is still on your deck.',
    'dx.repo/fire.why': 'The company is in no shape to start a fight with the Ninth Lance.',
    'dx.battle#name': 'The Double-Cross',
    'dx.battle': 'The orbital’s docking ring is two rings of deck joined by spokes over open space, cargo stacks, fuel tanks and the traffic tower at its hub, under heavy spin gravity that makes every step a labour. The Ninth Lance is small, expensive and very good: every unit it has is better than the company’s best. Break them, and the log stays where it is.',
    'dx.debrief': '{if skip}Daw signs for the log, very properly, and casts off with it. Nobody fires a shot. Vane does not call.{/if}{if success}The Lance pulls back to its ship and casts off. Daw salutes from the airlock, which is either very professional or very insulting.{/if}{if failure}The Lance takes what it came for, and any lodestone it can find besides.{/if}',
    'dx.terms': 'Vane’s lawyer calls before the smoke has cleared the deck. Her client is prepared to be reasonable. Her client always is, when reason is cheaper.',
    'dx.terms/pay': 'Pay him for the log, and be his company again, on paper.',
    'dx.terms/pay.why': 'The company can’t pay him.',
    'dx.terms/tell': 'Tell him where to put his offer.',
    'dx.terms/offer': 'Offer him the next lodestone the company finds.',
    'dx.interlude': '{if log_handed}The company jumps on without the log and without a patron, with Halloran’s copies of its last pages and the stones it holds to go by. Every company on the trail has heard by now, and some of them have started calling.{/if}{if not log_handed}The company jumps on with the log and without a patron. Every company on the trail has heard by now, and some of them have started calling.{/if}',

    /* ================= between chapters ================= */
    'i.wake': 'The company buries its own, on a world none of them had heard of a month ago.',
    'i.wake/pay': 'Pay the death benefits in full, out of the company’s own pocket.',
    'i.wake/pay.why': 'The company can’t pay them.',
    'i.wake/dont': 'Put it off. The company needs the money for the next jump.',
    'i.sendahead': 'Halloran’s backer wants a lodestone sent ahead to his vault, now, by courier, to an address that changes twice before the courier lands. He doesn’t say why. He doesn’t have to.',
    'i.sendahead/send': 'Send it. He’s paying.',
    'i.sendahead/refuse': 'Refuse. The stones stay with the company until the end of the trail.',
    'i.voices': '{soldier:veteran} has started hearing them: voices, at night, when the lodestones are near. Not words. Something like a crowd, a long way off, all saying the same thing.',
    'i.voices/listen': 'Sit up with them, and listen.',
    'i.voices/home': 'Send them home, on the next ship, with full pay.',
    'i.warm': 'The lodestones are warm. All of them, in their case, and pointing the same way, like compass needles. Nobody has touched them.',
    'i.warm/follow': 'Follow where they point.',
    'i.warm/shield': 'Shield the case, and stop them calling to whatever they’re calling.',
    'i.warm/shield.why': 'The company can’t afford the shielding.',
    'i.newsfeed': 'Rook Kestrel calls in person, from a ship full of cameras. She wants pictures from the trail, and pays for them better than Halloran’s backer pays for anything.',
    'i.newsfeed/sell': 'Sell them pictures. Leave out anything that matters.',
    'i.newsfeed/refuse': 'Refuse. Nobody on the trail needs to see where the company has been.',
    'i.catalogue': 'Moss Kettering has not dropped out of the race; he is hedging it. Brass Ledger make money either way, and they are selling everything they have learned about the trail and the companies on it, in a neat catalogue, to anyone who pays. There is a page about {name} in it, and Kettering would like to buy a few more.',
    'i.catalogue/buy': 'Buy the catalogue, and see what Brass Ledger know about everyone else.',
    'i.catalogue/buy.why': 'The company can’t afford it.',
    'i.catalogue/sell': 'Sell Kettering a page or two about the company’s own trail. The money is good; Halloran’s backer will wonder where it went.',
    'i.catalogue/ignore': 'Ignore it. Let Kettering sell it to someone else.',
    'i.tired': 'The company is tired, and it is starting to say so. {soldier:veteran} asks, for all of them, for a week off before the next jump.',
    'i.tired/rest': 'Put in somewhere quiet for a week, and lose the time.',
    'i.tired/bonus': 'Pay them a bonus, and keep going.',
    'i.tired/bonus.why': 'There is no money for a bonus.',
    'i.tired/push': 'Not yet. Push on.',
    'i.jump': 'The jump drive is failing, slowly, the way old drives do. There is a yard on the way, and the yard has a price.',
    'i.jump/yard': 'Put in at the yard and pay for the work.',
    'i.jump/yard.why': 'The company can’t pay the yard.',
    'i.jump/jump': 'Jump anyway, and trust the engineers.',

    /* ---- the hired guns ---- */
    'hired.invoice': 'Brass Ledger’s invoice arrives before the dust settles, and it is for more than was agreed: hazard pay, ammunition, a line marked “sundries”.',
    'hired.invoice/pay': 'Pay it. Good help costs.',
    'hired.invoice/pay.why': 'The company can’t pay it.',
    'hired.invoice/dispute': 'Dispute every line.',
    'hired.invoice/share': 'Offer them a share of the claim at the end instead.',
    'hired.edit': 'Kestrel’s cameras were rolling the whole time. Rook wants to cut the footage her way, and her way makes Kestrel the heroes.',
    'hired.edit/theirs': 'Let her cut it. Pictures are her business.',
    'hired.edit/raw': 'Demand the raw footage first.',
    'hired.edit/interview': 'Give her an interview instead: the company, in its own words.',
    'hired.mess': 'The Red Reavers have left a mess where they fought: the dead stripped, the wounded left, and something on fire that should not be.',
    'hired.mess/pay': 'Pay them off and walk away from it.',
    'hired.mess/pay.why': 'The company can’t pay them.',
    'hired.mess/clean': 'Make them clean it up, at gunpoint if it comes to that.',
    'hired.mess/look': 'Look the other way. They fought well.',
    'hired.favour': 'Colonel Sable sends word: the favour is paid, and Sable Armour Group owes the company nothing more.',
    'hired.favour/thank': 'Thank her properly, and mean it.',
    'hired.favour/again': 'Ask whether she’d do it again.',
    'hired.kettleoffer': 'Moss Kettering calls, not in the least apologetic: someone has offered Brass Ledger more than the company is paying, and business is business. Match it by the end of the day, or the next time his lads see the company it will be over their sights.',
    'hired.kettleoffer/match': 'Match the offer.',
    'hired.kettleoffer/match.why': 'The company can’t match it.',
    'hired.kettleoffer/let': 'Let them go to whoever it is.',
    'hired.reaversoffer': 'Big Hal has had a better offer, and is honest enough to say so, which for Hal is a kind of loyalty.',
    'hired.reaversoffer/match': 'Match it.',
    'hired.reaversoffer/match.why': 'The company can’t match it.',
    'hired.reaversoffer/let': 'Let them go.',

    /* ================= the second half: The Long Jump ================= */
    'fault': 'Halloran sat up three nights with the last pages of the log, the ones Morrow wrote with the stones all pointing the same way, and on the fourth morning he read them out to the company. Morrow had a name for the ones who keep the stones after all. He wrote it once, near the end, underlined twice: Xenotripods. The three-legged ones, who step out of the air. By the next jump every newsfeed in the sector has the word, and the footage from the beacon, and nobody is calling it a treasure hunt any more.',
    'fault.brief': 'The log gives no more easy headings. From here the stones point, and the company chooses which way to follow them, knowing now who else is out there. The rivals have heard the same newsfeeds. So have their clients.{if vane_cut_off} And Vane has stopped paying.{/if}',

    /* ---- the second half's jobs, as they are put to the company at the pool ---- */
    'claim.pitch': 'The stones point at the Hollin Shelf, where Morrow left something behind before he went on. Kestrel’s cameras are already up there.',
    'wreck.pitch': 'A wreck in a gully on Pell matches nothing on any register. Halloran is sure it is Morrow’s ship. So is Colonel Sable, and she is already cutting in.',
    'bounty.pitch': 'Somebody has put a price on the company, and Wraith Section has taken the contract. There is fuel on Ilya, and a tip that they are waiting there.',
    'watchers.pitch': 'The stones point through the Brannock pass, and something has shut the pass with walls of light that watch whoever comes near.',
    'storm.pitch': 'Nobody has slept well since the beacon. Make camp on Sedra II to refit, and be ready for whatever has been following the company there.',
    'cold.pitch': 'The stones point under the ruins on Kasimir, where the sand moves when nothing is moving it.',
    'mimics.pitch': 'A call from Hashamer I, in a voice that is not human and asks politely: somebody is taking its people away in cages.{if eshaven_graves} It gives two names the company has read on a cairn of glass.{/if}',
    'speaker.pitch': 'On the shore of a still lake on Orlo, something has been waiting for the company to come and talk. It has been waiting patiently.',
    'signal.pitch': 'Go back to the beacon on Tessaly IV, where it started. Light it, and hold it lit, and see who answers.',
    'vault.pitch': 'Halcyon, and Vane’s banks, and under one of them the vault where he keeps everything he has bought and the receipts for everything he has sold.',
    'escort.pitch': 'Vane wants a cargo moved across Umber, quietly, and he is paying as if it matters a great deal.',
    'debts.pitch': 'Vane’s lawyers say the company owes him, and the Ninth Lance has been sent to collect. They will find the company whichever way it goes.',

    /* ---- the Race: Claim Jumpers ---- */
    'claim': 'The stones point at the Hollin Shelf, a high plateau on Corvin where Morrow made a cache before he went on. Kestrel Air Cavalry got the heading the same day the company did, and Kestrel is never second anywhere.',
    'claim.brief': 'Kestrel’s dropships are already on the shelf, and so is a convoy of ground cars carrying whatever Morrow cached, crawling for Kestrel’s landing zone in the thin air. Rook Kestrel is filming the whole thing for somebody’s newsfeed.',
    'claim.scoop': 'Rook Kestrel comes up on the open channel, cheerful, with a camera drone over her shoulder. The cache is Kestrel’s, the pictures are her client’s, and the company can watch from a respectful distance. Or the company can try to take the convoy, on camera, and be the villains of the evening news.',
    'claim.scoop/race': 'Race them for the convoy, and let her film it.',
    'claim.scoop/pictures': 'Offer her better pictures than a firefight: the company’s own footage of the beacon.',
    'claim.scoop/hire_kestrel': 'Pay Kestrel for a share of the cache, and ride shotgun on the convoy. The Red Reavers are out on the shelf too, and they jump claims for a living.',
    'claim.battle#name': 'Claim Jumpers',
    'claim.battle': '{if claim_escort}Morrow’s cache is on Kestrel’s ground cars, crossing the shelf for their landing zone, and the company is riding with them. The Red Reavers are waiting somewhere along the track: claim jumpers, and proud of it. Get Kestrel’s cars across. The thin air and the low gravity make every jump longer and every fall worse.{/if}{if not claim_escort}Morrow’s cache is on Kestrel’s ground cars, crossing the shelf for their landing zone. Stop the convoy and take what it carries, before it lifts off. Kestrel is fast, airborne, and always in the wrong place for you; the thin air and the low gravity make every jump longer and every fall worse.{/if}',
    'claim.debrief': '{if success+!claim_escort}The convoy is stopped on the shelf, and Morrow’s cache is the company’s: a strongbox of his notes, and a lodestone wrapped in a flag from a survey service that no longer exists.{/if}{if failure+!claim_escort}Kestrel’s dropships lift off the shelf with the cache aboard, and Rook waves from the ramp.{/if}{if claim_escort+success}Kestrel’s cars roll up the ramps with the cache aboard, and Rook keeps her side of the deal on camera: the company’s share is a lodestone, wrapped in a flag from a survey service that no longer exists.{/if}{if claim_escort+failure}The Reavers have the cache, and Kestrel’s cars are burning on the shelf. Rook films that too.{/if}',
    'claim.footage': 'Kestrel filmed all of it. Rook sends a cut over before it airs, as a courtesy: it is very good, and the company does not come out of it well.',
    'claim.footage/run': 'Let them run it. Pictures are her business.',
    'claim.footage/buy': 'Buy the footage before it airs.',
    'claim.footage/buy.why': 'The company can’t afford it.',
    'claim.interlude': 'The company jumps off the shelf. Every newsfeed in the sector is running the Hollin Shelf now, one way or another.',

    /* ---- the Race: The Wreck of the Morrow ---- */
    'wreck': 'The stones point at Pell, a poisoned world with a toxic sky, and at a wreck in a gully that matches nothing on any register: Elias Morrow’s survey ship, where he brought her down for the last time. Sable Armour Group got there first, because Sable always digs in first.',
    'wreck.brief': 'Morrow’s ship lies on her side in a gully under a yellow sky, hull split, half full of drifted ash. Sable’s vehicles ring her. Somewhere inside is the end of Morrow’s story, and possibly Morrow.',
    'wreck.hatch': 'Colonel Sable is cutting into the wreck through the main hatch, slowly, by the book. {if beat_sable}She has seen the company’s ship come down, and has doubled her pickets. Kerrigan, she says on the open channel, will not happen twice.{/if}{if not beat_sable}Her message to the company is short: the wreck is her client’s salvage, and anyone who comes within a kilometre of it will be fired on.{/if}',
    'wreck.hatch/cut': 'Cut a second way in, through the split in the hull, and get to the bridge first.',
    'wreck.hatch/wait': 'Wait for Sable to open her up, and take it from her inside.',
    'wreck.hatch/hire_sable': 'Call in what Sable owes the company, and go in together.',
    'wreck.battle#name': 'The Wreck of the Morrow',
    'wreck.battle': '{if wreck_together}Sable’s people and the company go in together by the main hatch, and find Brass Ledger already inside: a salvage crew that came in through the split in the hull and means to strip her to the frames. Clear them out of Morrow’s ship, room by room. The air outside is poison; the air inside is not much better, and every bulkhead is a place for one of Kettering’s guns to hold.{/if}{if not wreck_together}The wreck is corridors and holds lying on their side, ash to the knee, and Sable’s people in all of them. Clear them out of Morrow’s ship, room by room. The air outside is poison; the air inside is not much better, and every bulkhead is a place for one of Sable’s guns to hold.{/if}',
    'wreck.debrief': '{if success+!wreck_together}Sable’s people are out of the wreck, and the company has it to itself: the bridge, the holds, and a cabin sealed from the inside nearly forty years ago.{/if}{if failure+!wreck_together}Sable holds the wreck, and whatever Morrow left in it goes to her client’s vaults.{/if}{if wreck_together+success}Brass Ledger’s salvage crew is out of the wreck, and Sable keeps her word: the holds are hers, the bridge is the company’s, and so is a cabin sealed from the inside nearly forty years ago.{/if}{if wreck_together+failure}Brass Ledger holds the wreck, and Sable pulls her people out. Whatever Morrow left in it is on Kettering’s manifest now.{/if}',
    'wreck.morrow': 'Elias Morrow is in his cabin, in his chair, nearly forty years dead and dry as paper. His last entry is on the desk in front of him, unfinished: “His ship is in orbit. It has been in orbit for a week, waiting for me to come out. I will not. Let him have the hull. He will not have this one, and he will not have the others, and if anyone reads this who is not his: never two in one hold. They are not a key. They are a summons.” In his fist, still warm, is a lodestone.',
    'wreck.morrow/take': 'Take the stone, and his last entry.',
    'wreck.morrow/bury': 'Bury him on Pell, with the stone in his hand where he wanted it.',
    'wreck.interlude': 'The company lifts off Pell with Morrow’s last words aboard, or without them. Either way, his ship is empty now.',

    /* ---- the Race: Bounty ---- */
    'bounty': 'Somebody has put a price on the company. Wraith Section took the contract, and Wraith Section does not miss twice. The company is on Ilya, a nearly empty world, to refuel, and a tip arrives an hour after landing.',
    'bounty.brief': 'Ilya is scrub and wind and a single fuel depot. Wraith Section has been paid to stop the company here, and the tip says they are already in the hills.',
    'bounty.tip': 'The tip comes from Nine herself, on a channel nobody should have. {if beat_wraith}“This one is not for the client,” she says. “This one is for Vantor.”{/if}{if not beat_wraith}“It’s a contract,” she says. “You understand. Leave the stones on the pad and we can all be somewhere else by sunset.”{/if}',
    'bounty.tip/trap': 'Lay a trap for the trap: let them think the company is still refuelling.',
    'bounty.tip/trap.why': 'There is no Intel to spend on it.',
    'bounty.tip/run': 'Run for the far side of the depot and make them come to you.',
    'bounty.tip/deal': 'Take Nine’s offer: a lodestone, and she calls it off.',
    'bounty.tip/deal.why': 'Nine has no reason to talk to the company, and nothing to sell.',
    'bounty.tip/hire_reavers': 'Hire the Reavers to hunt the hunters.',
    'bounty.battle#name': 'Bounty',
    'bounty.battle': 'Wraith Section is in the hills over the depot with long rifles and every channel tapped. Break them, and the bounty goes unpaid. They will shoot officers and radios first, and they will not come close if they can help it.',
    'bounty.debrief': '{if skip}Nine takes the lodestone, and Wraith Section is gone from the hills by sunset. The bounty is called off. For now.{/if}{if success}Wraith Section breaks contact and is gone into the scrub. The bounty is unpaid, and somebody will be unhappy about that.{/if}{if failure}Wraith Section collects. The company gets off Ilya lighter than it landed.{/if}',
    'bounty.paymaster': 'Wraith left someone behind: the contract’s paymaster, a neat man with a case of bearer bonds and a very good lawyer’s number.',
    'bounty.paymaster/hand': 'Hand him in, and send Vane the case.',
    'bounty.paymaster/hand.why': 'Vane has cut the company off. There is nobody to send the case to.',
    'bounty.paymaster/turn': 'Turn him: who pays Wraith, and how much they know.',
    'bounty.interlude': 'The company jumps on, watching its own tail all the way.',

    /* ---- the Race: The Landing (finale) ---- */
    'landing': 'The end of the log. The stones point at a world where the dead reckoning says there should be nothing at all, and there is the Quiet: a world of towers under a slow fog, and one landing field. The leading company in the race got here first. Whoever files the first claim owns everything here.',
    'landing.brief': 'The only landing field on the Quiet is dug in from end to end, and the rival who holds it has been waiting for the company for days. Their commander is on the field. Take the field, and the claim is the company’s to file.',
    'landing.field': 'They are dug in, and they have heard the company’s drives. There is no message on the open channel this time. There doesn’t need to be.',
    'landing.field/alone': 'Go in alone.',
    'landing.field/hire_kettle': 'Hire Brass Ledger for the landing.',
    'landing.field/hire_reavers': 'Hire the Reavers for the landing.',
    'landing.field/hire_kettle.why': 'Brass Ledger won’t fight for the company here: either they have no reason to, or they are the ones dug in on the field.',
    'landing.field/hire_reavers.why': 'The Reavers won’t fight for the company here: either they have no reason to, or they are the ones dug in on the field.',
    'landing.battle#name': 'The Landing',
    'landing.battle': 'The landing field on the Quiet, and the leading rival dug in across it. Take it from them, or take their commander: either way, the claim is the company’s. The towers are watching. Everything on this world is.',
    'landing.debrief': '{if success}The field is the company’s, and the claim is filed before the guns have cooled.{/if}{if failure}The rival holds the field, and files the claim in its client’s name before the company is off the ground.{/if}',

    /* ---- the Tribes: The Watchers ---- */
    'watchers': 'The stones point through the Brannock pass, and the pass is shut. The Ulvar, a Xenotripod tribe of shields and guns, have domed it from wall to wall. Nobody has gone through it since the word got out.',
    'watchers.brief': 'Brannock VI is mountains and fog. The Ulvar’s shield domes sit across the only pass like soap bubbles of light, each held up by a turret, and the turrets watch everything.',
    'watchers.wall': 'The shieldwall covers the pass from side to side. The Ulvar have seen the company. They do not move, and they do not need to.',
    'watchers.wall/strike': 'Strike the shield turrets, and bring the domes down.',
    'watchers.wall/round': 'Go round, the long way, over the peaks.',
    'watchers.wall/under': 'Go under it, by the tunnels the Mithdu showed the company.',
    'watchers.wall/under.why': 'Nobody has shown the company a way under.',
    'watchers.battle#name': 'The Watchers',
    'watchers.battle': 'The Ulvar’s shield turrets hold the pass, each under its own dome, with guns behind every one. Destroy the turrets and the domes come down. The Ulvar fight from behind their shields and do not give ground.',
    'watchers.debrief': '{if skip}The company goes round, the long way, over the peaks. The Ulvar watch it go from behind their domes, and do not follow.{/if}{if success}The domes are down, and the pass is open. The Ulvar have drawn back up the mountain.{/if}{if failure}The shieldwall holds. The company goes round, the long way, and the Ulvar know its face now.{/if}',
    'watchers.dome': 'The last dome comes down. Its keepers stand in the open, unarmed, and wait. In every head at once: they want their dead back.',
    'watchers.dome/give': 'Give them back their dead.',
    'watchers.dome/cores': 'Keep the turret cores. They are worth a fortune to the right buyer.',
    'watchers.interlude': 'The company goes through the pass. Behind it, the Ulvar begin to rebuild the wall.',

    /* ---- the Tribes: Teleport Storm ---- */
    'storm': 'The Sh’kar have not forgotten the beacon. They come for the company on Sedra II, where it has made camp to refit, and they do not come by the road.',
    'storm.brief': 'Sedra II is open plain under a sky that does strange things to time. The camp’s sensors start screaming an hour before dawn: the air itself is folding, everywhere, and something is about to step through it.',
    'storm.air': 'The air goes wrong around the camp: shimmers, then holes, then light. The Sh’kar are coming through, and the company has minutes.',
    'storm.air/dig': 'Dig in where the company stands, and hold until they stop coming.',
    'storm.air/move': 'Move the lodestones away from the camp, and draw them off.',
    'storm.battle#name': 'Teleport Storm',
    'storm.battle': 'The Sh’kar are stepping out of the air all round the camp, and they will keep coming. Hold the camp. They come for the stones, and for the people who took them.',
    'storm.debrief': '{if success}The air closes. The Sh’kar are gone, and the camp is still standing, more or less.{/if}{if failure}The Sh’kar take what they came for, and step back into the air with it.{/if}',
    'storm.what': 'In the quiet after, the company understands what the Sh’kar came for: not the company. The lodestones.',
    'storm.what/return': 'Return one. Leave it on the plain, where they can find it.',
    'storm.what/return.why': 'The company has no stones to return.',
    'storm.what/keep': 'Keep them all.',
    'storm.interlude': 'The company jumps on, and nobody sleeps much for the first few nights.',

    /* ---- the Tribes: Cold Sites ---- */
    'cold': 'The stones point at Kasimir, a desert world full of old ruins that nobody has dug, and for good reason. The Mithdu live under them.',
    'cold.brief': 'The ruins on Kasimir are half buried in sand that moves when nothing is moving it. The Mithdu are underneath, and the stones say the way on is underneath too.',
    'cold.sand': 'The sand moves. The ruins are not empty, and whatever is in them has noticed the company.',
    'cold.sand/clear': 'Clear the ruins.',
    'cold.sand/seal': 'Seal them, and go round.',
    'cold.battle#name': 'Cold Sites',
    'cold.battle': 'The ruins are corridors and rooms half full of sand, and the Mithdu come out of the walls and up through the floors. Clear them, room by room. Keep your back to something solid, if you can find anything solid.',
    'cold.debrief': '{if skip}The company seals the ruins with charges and goes round. The sand closes over them behind it.{/if}{if success}The ruins are clear, and the way on is open.{/if}{if failure}The Mithdu keep their ruins. The company goes the long way round.{/if}',
    'cold.child': 'In the last room, in the sand, a Mithdu child. It hasn’t run. It is watching the company with all of its eyes.',
    'cold.child/let': 'Let it go.',
    'cold.child/vane': 'Take it to Vane. He will pay anything for one alive.',
    'cold.child/vane.why': 'Vane has cut the company off. He will not take its calls, whatever it has to sell.',
    'cold.interlude': 'The company leaves Kasimir. The sand closes over the ruins behind it.',

    /* ---- the Tribes: The Spire (finale) ---- */
    'spire': 'The end of the log is the Quiet, and the Quiet is the Amt: towers that rule a world, asleep, and waking. The tribes are gathering. The company is going into the tallest tower, to put out whatever is waking them.',
    'spire.brief': 'The spire stands over the fog, sealed, humming, its shield generators burning in chambers all the way up. Burn them out, and the spire sleeps. Fail, and every tribe the company has met is coming home to it.',
    'spire.door': 'The spire is sealed: a door with no seam, that knows the company is there.',
    'spire.door/force': 'Burn the way in.',
    'spire.door/eshaven': 'Let the Esh-Aven open it. They remember how.',
    'spire.door/eshaven.why': 'Nobody here will open it for the company.',
    'spire.door/quiet': 'Go in quietly, and find another way.',
    'spire.battle#name': 'The Spire',
    'spire.battle': 'The spire is corridors and halls, and its shield generators burn in chambers of their own. Burn every one of them out. The Amt are waking, and their servants come out of the walls to stop the company.',
    'spire.debrief': '{if success}The last generator gutters out, and the spire’s hum drops to nothing. The Quiet is quiet again.{/if}{if failure}The company is driven out of the spire, and behind it the towers light, one by one.{/if}',

    /* ---- First Contact: The Mimics ---- */
    'mimics': 'Hashamer I is a desert world of nine million Esh-Aven, the servant race of the Xenotripods, left behind when their masters went home to the Quiet. They have kept their masters’ war going ever since by copying it. And now somebody is selling them.',
    'mimics.brief': 'A village of low stone houses on the edge of the Hashamer desert, and on the ridge above it, Brass Ledger’s trucks: cages on the back, and a buyer’s manifest in Kettering’s pocket.',
    'mimics.ask': 'An Esh-Aven comes down from the village alone, wearing an Alpha’s gold crest that was never made for it, and stops at a polite distance from the company’s guns. It gives its name, Seven-Holds-the-Staff, as if that were the most important thing about it. One of the other hands came before, it says, a long time ago: a man alone with a hold of stones, carrying them away from the Quiet, and two of the Hashamer went with him to carry them, Holds-the-Lamp and First-Over-the-Water. They did not come home.{if eshaven_graves} The company has seen where they lie.{/if} Then it asks the company, the other hands, to fight for it.',
    'mimics.ask/help': 'Fight beside them.',
    'mimics.ask/pay': 'Pay Brass Ledger to take their trucks and go.',
    'mimics.ask/pay.why': 'Brass Ledger won’t deal with the company.',
    'mimics.battle#name': 'The Mimics',
    'mimics.battle': 'Brass Ledger is coming down off the ridge for the village, and the Hashamer will fight beside the company: every one of them an Esh-Aven, every one named. Drive the slavers off. Kettering is here for a manifest, and he means to fill it.',
    'mimics.debrief': '{if skip}Brass Ledger take the money and drive their trucks back up the ridge, the cages empty. They will be back when somebody pays more.{/if}{if success}Brass Ledger’s trucks are going back up the ridge empty. In the village, the Esh-Aven are saying the names of their dead aloud, one by one: {fallen:eshaven}.{/if}{if failure}Brass Ledger fills its cages. The company could not stop it.{/if}',
    'mimics.thanks': 'Seven-Holds-the-Staff comes to the company’s fire that night. The Hashamer remember the road their masters took to the Quiet, it says: every turn of it, in their blood. They will guide the company there. They ask one thing in return. When the company reaches the Quiet, it must not wake it, for if it wakes, it will call every Esh-Aven home to serve again.',
    'mimics.thanks/promise': 'Make the promise.',
    'mimics.thanks/guide': 'Take the guide, and promise nothing.',
    'mimics.thanks/stone': 'Ask for a lodestone instead.',
    'mimics.interlude': 'The company jumps on, with an Esh-Aven guide in the navigator’s chair, or without one.',

    /* ---- First Contact: The Speaker ---- */
    'speaker': 'On Orlo, on the far shore of a lake as still as glass, an Alpha is waiting. Not hiding, not guarding: waiting, beside a pylon of its people’s make, for the company to come and talk.',
    'speaker.brief': 'Orlo is grass, lake and sky. The Alpha stands by its pylon on the far shore with a handful of its own, weapons grounded. In every head at once, as the company lands: a word that means come.',
    'speaker.pylon': 'The pylon hums. It is waiting for the company, and so is the Alpha.',
    'speaker.pylon/meet': 'Meet it.',
    'speaker.pylon/vane': 'Bring Vane’s people to the meeting.',
    'speaker.pylon/vane.why': 'Vane has cut the company off.',
    'speaker.battle#name': 'The Speaker',
    'speaker.battle': 'Talks on the shore, under a flag of the company’s own making. The Alpha speaks through Mental Projection, into every head at once, and it has things to say about the beacon and the stones. Come to an understanding, or it comes to a fight.',
    'speaker.debrief': '{if success}The Alpha’s last words are in a human voice, learned from the company over a single afternoon: “We will remember the other hands.”{/if}{if failure}The talks are over, and so is the quiet on Orlo.{/if}',
    'speaker.interlude': 'The company jumps on. Some of it is still hearing the Alpha’s voice, a long way off, like a crowd saying the same thing.',

    /* ---- First Contact: Signal Fires ---- */
    'signal': 'Back to Tessaly IV, and the beacon, where the company first saw them. This time the company has come to light it, and to hold it lit until whoever is listening hears.',
    'signal.brief': 'The beacon on Tessaly IV, still humming in the fog. Lit, it will call. The Sh’kar who keep it will try to stop the company lighting it, and keep trying until it is done.',
    'signal.battle#name': 'Signal Fires',
    'signal.battle': 'Hold the line around the beacon while it is lit and calling. The Sh’kar will keep coming out of the fog and the air, wave after wave. Hold until the signal is sent.',
    'signal.debrief': '{if success}The beacon burns, and somewhere a long way off, something answers.{/if}{if failure}The company is driven off the island. The beacon goes dark behind it.{/if}',
    'signal.interlude': 'The company jumps on. Behind it, on Tessaly IV, the beacon is lit or it is not.',

    /* ---- the Patron: The Vault ---- */
    'vault': 'Halcyon is a world of banks, and Lucan Vane owns three of them. Under one of them is a vault, and in the vault, if the rumours are right, is everything Vane has ever bought: relics, and receipts for what he has sold.',
    'vault.brief': 'The Halcyon vault sits under a bank tower in the middle of the city, behind the Ninth Lance. Nobody has seen inside it. Nobody has seen Vane, either.',
    'vault.door': 'Vane’s vault. His relics, the rumour says, and receipts for what he has sold, and to whom.',
    'vault.door/night': 'Break in by night.',
    'vault.door/ask': 'Ask Vane to see it.',
    'vault.door/ask.why': 'Vane won’t see the company. Vane doesn’t see anyone.',
    'vault.battle#name': 'The Vault',
    'vault.battle': 'The vault is corridors and strongrooms under the bank, and the Ninth Lance holds every one. Take the vault’s control room and hold it. The Lance is few, expensive, and very good.',
    'vault.debrief': '{if skip}Vane opens the vault himself, for an hour, and stands in the doorway while the company looks.{/if}{if success}The vault is open, and the company is standing in it.{/if}{if failure}The Lance holds the vault. Whatever is in it stays there.{/if}',
    'vault.receipts': 'The vault holds lodestones, a dozen of them, and receipts. Vane has been selling stones to the rival companies all along: a few to each, enough to keep the race running. He paid for every company on the trail, one way or another: the Halvorsen Combine and the Meridian newsfeed are his, through Halcyon, and so was Halloran’s money. Brass Ledger he never needed to own: Kettering sold him every page they learned. At the bottom of the box is the oldest receipt of all, forty years old, made out to a newly founded Ninth Lance: a man to be found, past the edge of the charts.',
    'vault.receipts/publish': 'Publish them.',
    'vault.receipts/use': 'Keep them, and use them.',
    'vault.interlude': 'The company leaves Halcyon with the vault’s secrets, or without them. Vane, on the line, says nothing at all.',

    /* ---- the Patron: The Escort ---- */
    'escort': 'Vane wants a cargo of relics moved across Umber, a poisoned industrial world, to a ship that will take them somewhere nobody will ask. The Navy wants it too: Wraith Section has an Admiralty warrant to impound anything that came off the trail, and Umber’s refinery belt is a long road.',
    'escort.brief': 'Vane’s relics are in sealed containers on three trucks. The field is on the far side of Umber’s refinery belt, and Wraith Section is somewhere between here and there.',
    'escort.road': 'Two roads to the field: the fast one, through the refineries, and the long one round them.',
    'escort.road/fast': 'The fast road.',
    'escort.road/long': 'The long road.',
    'escort.battle#name': 'The Escort',
    'escort.battle': 'Get Vane’s convoy across Umber to the field. Wraith Section is waiting in the refinery belt with long rifles and the Admiralty’s warrant, and they will go for the trucks.',
    'escort.debrief': '{if success}The convoy is through, and Vane’s relics are on the ship.{/if}{if failure}Wraith Section takes the convoy, under the Admiralty’s warrant. Vane will want to know why.{/if}',
    'escort.cargo': 'Vane’s relics, safe, and one of the containers is not quite sealed.',
    'escort.cargo/deliver': 'Deliver it all.',
    'escort.cargo/keep': 'Keep a stone.',
    'escort.interlude': 'The company jumps on, a little richer, or a little less trusted.',

    /* ---- the Patron: Debts ---- */
    'debts': 'On Tarn, a refinery world, the bailiffs find the company. They are the Ninth Lance, with paperwork: Vane says the company owes him, and the Lance has come to collect.',
    'debts.brief': 'The Lance has the company’s ship under a court order, and the company outside it.',
    'debts.bailiffs': 'Commander Daw, with a writ and a very calm voice. {if beat_lance}“We have met before,” she says. “I have not forgotten it. Pay, or don’t. I would rather you didn’t.”{/if}{if not beat_lance}“Pay the debt, and we all go home. Don’t, and I take it out of the company’s hide. It is only business.”{/if}',
    'debts.bailiffs/pay': 'Pay them.',
    'debts.bailiffs/pay.why': 'The company can’t pay them.',
    'debts.bailiffs/fight': 'Fight them.',
    'debts.battle#name': 'Debts',
    'debts.battle': 'The Ninth Lance is between the company and its ship, with a court order and very good guns. Break them, and the debt is a matter for lawyers.',
    'debts.debrief': '{if skip}The company pays the writ in full, and the Lance stands down. Daw gives a receipt.{/if}{if success}The Lance pulls back, and the writ is so much paper.{/if}{if failure}The Lance collects, in full.{/if}',
    'debts.interlude': 'The company lifts off Tarn, and Vane’s lawyers start writing letters.',

    /* ---- the Patron: The Collector (finale) ---- */
    'collector': 'The Quiet, at last, and its one landing field. The Ninth Lance holds it. And for the first time, Lucan Vane is in the room: a tall man in a grey coat, on the field, in person, come to collect what he paid for.',
    'collector.brief': 'Vane’s flag flies over the landing field, and the Ninth Lance is dug in round it. Vane himself is there. Nobody in the company has ever seen his face. He has never needed them to.',
    'collector.vane': 'For the first time, Vane is in the room. He is older than his voice, and smaller, and entirely calm. He has terms.',
    'collector.vane/terms': 'Hear his terms.',
    'collector.vane/terms.why': 'He owes the company nothing, and he knows it.',
    'collector.vane/fight': 'Shoot the flag down.',
    'collector.vane/hire_reavers': 'Hire the Reavers against him.',
    'collector.battle#name': 'The Collector',
    'collector.battle': 'The landing field on the Quiet, the Ninth Lance dug in across it, and Commander Daw in the middle. Take the field or take Daw, and the Quiet is not Vane’s.',
    'collector.debrief': '{if success}The Lance is broken, and Vane’s flag comes down. He walks back up the ramp of his ship without a word, and nobody tries to stop him.{/if}{if failure}The field is Vane’s, and so is the Quiet.{/if}',

    /* ---- Recall ---- */
    'recall': 'Every teleport turret on the Quiet opens at once. The towers are awake, and they are calling every one of their people home.',
    'recall.brief': 'The fog is full of light, and the light is full of Xenotripods, stepping through from everywhere. The landing field is the last way off.',
    'recall.run': 'The field is a mile off through the fog, and the air between is opening.',
    'recall.run/stones': 'Run with the stones.',
    'recall.run/stones.why': 'The company has no stones left to carry.',
    'recall.run/leave': 'Run, and leave the stones behind.',
    'recall.battle#name': 'Recall',
    'recall.battle': 'Break out across the field to the ships, before the Quiet’s tribes close it. They will keep coming out of the air until the last ship is gone.',
    'recall.debrief': 'The company is off the Quiet, with what it could carry. Behind it the towers are bright, and calling.',
    'end': 'The end of the log.'
  };
})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : global);
